WEBVTT

00:00:08.973 --> 00:00:12.440
We will connect a reservation button to an ordinary

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.752
Java object and its visible count.

00:00:14.752 --> 00:00:18.219
Watch how an action changes the data, refreshes the

00:00:18.219 --> 00:00:21.618
label, and controls whether another reservation is

00:00:21.618 --> 00:00:22.298
available.

00:00:56.567 --> 00:01:00.072
The model is the object that holds application state:

00:01:00.072 --> 00:01:01.857
SeatCounter owns remaining.

00:01:01.857 --> 00:01:04.899
The view displays that state through controls.

00:01:04.899 --> 00:01:08.205
A Button is a control that requests an action when

00:01:08.205 --> 00:01:08.866
activated.

00:01:08.866 --> 00:01:12.238
Here the Label displays the model’s count, and VBox

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:14.817
places the label and button vertically.

00:01:15.212 --> 00:01:18.649
An action event is a notification that the button’s

00:01:18.649 --> 00:01:20.402
requested action occurred.

00:01:20.402 --> 00:01:23.907
Event-handler registration connects behavior to that

00:01:23.907 --> 00:01:24.783
future event.

00:01:24.783 --> 00:01:28.220
setOnAction registers our lambda; registration does

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:29.366
not run its body.

00:01:29.366 --> 00:01:33.006
The lambda keeps references to the model and controls.

00:01:33.006 --> 00:01:36.646
Those local references stay the same while the objects

00:01:36.646 --> 00:01:37.387
can change.

00:01:37.504 --> 00:01:40.812
Predict the initial label and its value after three

00:01:40.812 --> 00:01:41.590
activations.

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:44.120
What should happen on a fourth attempt?

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.299
Trace the model update, label refresh, and button

00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:48.790
setting before running.

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:49.504
Pause here.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:03.108
The window starts at Remaining three.

00:02:03.108 --> 00:02:06.495
Counter ready three is only the console’s initial

00:02:06.495 --> 00:02:06.979
report.

00:02:06.979 --> 00:02:09.329
The label will show later changes.

00:02:09.329 --> 00:02:12.854
This standalone Application uses start to build the

00:02:12.854 --> 00:02:16.172
interface; notebook examples use the supplied Fx

00:02:16.172 --> 00:02:16.725
support.

00:02:19.004 --> 00:02:20.844
The first click leaves two.

00:02:20.844 --> 00:02:24.387
An event-driven update first changes the model, then

00:02:24.387 --> 00:02:26.839
setText refreshes the visible label.

00:02:26.839 --> 00:02:30.110
Keyboard focus selects the control that receives

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:31.132
keyboard input.

00:02:31.132 --> 00:02:34.675
Use Tab to focus this button, then Space to activate

00:02:34.675 --> 00:02:34.879
it.

00:02:37.069 --> 00:02:39.478
The keyboard activation leaves one.

00:02:39.478 --> 00:02:42.094
Activate again and check the boundary.

00:02:47.475 --> 00:02:50.622
Zero remains after further pointer and keyboard

00:02:50.622 --> 00:02:51.224
attempts.

00:02:51.224 --> 00:02:54.371
Control enablement allows or prevents an action

00:02:54.371 --> 00:02:55.643
according to state.

00:02:55.643 --> 00:02:59.259
setDisable blocks this button at zero, and the model’s

00:02:59.259 --> 00:03:01.803
own condition also protects its count.

00:03:01.803 --> 00:03:05.418
The event handler runs this short update on the JavaFX

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:08.900
Application Thread, the sequence for interface work.

00:03:10.866 --> 00:03:14.051
If you removed only the label refresh, which state

00:03:14.051 --> 00:03:17.172
would still change and which display would become

00:03:17.172 --> 00:03:17.873
misleading?

00:03:17.873 --> 00:03:21.249
Explain why the model condition and button enablement

00:03:21.249 --> 00:03:22.841
serve different purposes.
